    "reasoning": "All 16 mathematical answers agree with their own captured Math Academy worked solutions. No submitted grades or earlier answers were provided; confirmation rests on the captured worked solutions. q-123435: Integrating componentwise gives e^{t+1} and -4 cos(3t), plus an arbitrary constant vector C. q-18279: det(A)=20 and cofactor C_{21}=4, so the inverse entry is 4/20=1/5. q-104439: Direct calculation confirms det(A)=k-4 and C_{22}=1; 1/(k-4)=-1/4 gives k=0, with nonzero determinant. q-3594: Completing the square gives 1-(x+4)^2, whose reciprocal square root integrates to arcsin(x+4)+C on -5<x<-3. q-7196: Integrating acceleration gives (t+C_1,2t+C_2); the initial velocity gives C_1=3 and C_2=1. q-51343: Integration gives (e^{2t}/2+C_1,e^{3t}/3+C_2); the initial condition gives C_1=e^2/2 and C_2=2e^3/3. q-31628: The velocity is (2t^4-3,-t+4), yielding (159,1) at t=3. q-83497: The velocity is (3(1+2t)^4/2-1/2,-(1-3t)^3+7), yielding (121,15) at t=1. q-31634: The velocity components factor as t^2(t-5) and (t+1)(t-5); their only common zero is t=5. q-83506: The velocity is (1-cos(2t),sin(2t)); both components vanish only at t=0 in the specified interval. q-7988: Substitution into sec^2(t)-tan^2(t)=1 gives x^2/36-y^2/7=1. The stray closing bracket in the source prose does not affect its mathematics. q-39996: The geometric sum with first term 3 and ratio 5 is 3(5^N-1)/4. q-73425: The first term is 384/4^3=6, so the sum is 2(4^N-1). q-9608: The requested quadratic Taylor approximation is 3+1/6-1/216=683/216, which rounds to 3.16204; this is the specified approximation, rather than the actual square root rounded to five places. q-126844: The first three nonzero Taylor terms are ln(6)+(x-2)/2-(x-2)^2/8. q-7217: Integrating the velocity gives (t^3-t^2+C_1,2t^4+C_2); the position at t=3 gives C_1=12 and C_2=-72. Selected values preserve the exact current choice strings; r versus r(t) is equivalent notation for the position vector.",
